00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:11.970
Aside from their
performance claims though,

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:15.660
one of AMD's biggest marketing
points for the 7000 series

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.060
is the improvements to
performance per watt

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.010
that they've made with RDNA 3.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.040
And they've done this in a
number of kind of cool ways.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.590
So first of all, they're
using a combination

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:29.370
of TSMC'S 5-nanometer
and 6-nanometer processes

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.890
to build the first ever chiplet GPU.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:33.180
There are some functions

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.190
that they can't move off of the main dye

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.710
like compute hardware, graphics hardware,

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.240
and display and media engines.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.750
That main dye is built on
TSMC'S 5-nanometer process

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.820
so they can pack as much as possible in,

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:45.720
and they really did.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:46.553
I mean,

00:05:46.553 --> 00:05:49.050
this thing's got literally
twice as many stream processors

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.090
as AMD's previous flagship card.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.970
But they were able to move
their GDDR6 controllers

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.620
and other IO functions

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.500
onto these either five for the XT

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:02.280
or six MCDs or memory cache dies.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.470
And the reasons for this are twofold.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.480
One is to save cost,

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.200
and two is to, well, mostly save cost,

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.480
because apparently these functions

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.850
don't really shrink that well.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:18.180
So it just wasn't worth it
from a cost perspective.

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:19.770
So that's the main reason, I guess.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:22.470
Either way, the bandwidth
between the GCDs,

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:23.303
that's the main one,

00:06:23.303 --> 00:06:26.400
and the MCDs is a whopping, what is this?

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.220
5.3 terabytes a second.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.000
So that's 900 gigs a second per MCD.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.950
Absolutely flippin' wild.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:36.360
One of the other big innovations

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.210
that AMD has introduced to
improve performance per watt

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.190
is decoupled clock speeds.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.860
So they are apparently running the shaders

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:48.120
and the front end on these
GPUs at different speeds.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.890
So the shaders are advertised

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.410
at a game clock of 2.3 gigahertz

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:53.370
while the front end

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.670
is expected to hit in
the neighborhood of 2.5.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.440
Is that for both cards

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.810
or are those clocks
different between the two?

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.270
Ah, yes, that's for the XTX.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:07.560
So the XT is in the 2
to 2.4 gigahertz range.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.300
The reason for doing this is apparently

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.480
that by backing off even
just part of the card,

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.620
a little bit off of that
voltage per frequency curve,

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:18.270
you can save a ton of power

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.360
without giving up too much
in terms of performance.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.960
AMD has also added dedicated
AI accelerators to these cards.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.500
So we're looking at two AI
accelerators per compute unit,

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.470
